one of the things that Donald Trump has  said a number of times now to try to  convince people that impeaching him is a  bad idea  is he's been saying the stock market  would crash if I got impeached now it's  not a crazy thing to suggest markets in  general don't like instability the  removal of a president or even the  attempted removal of a president is a  form of instability so it is reasonable  to think wow if Trump were impeached if  Trump were impeached and convicted for  removal the markets would absolutely  crash and Donald Trump has kept pushing  this lately he tweeted late last week  quote if they actually did this the  markets would crash do you think it was  luck that got us to the best stock  market and economy in our history it  wasn't so put aside for a second that  most stocks are held by relatively few  people richer people and so the level of  the stock market is not the best  indicator of the health of the economy  put that aside just for a second okay  there's a good case to be made that  markets would actually do really well  and might even soar rather than crash if  Donald Trump were impeached Rick Newman  from Yahoo Finance makes the case for  this and Rick points out that number one  markets have been relatively stable  since the impeachment inquiry was  announced you've actually seen very few  big swing days one way or the other  since the impeachment inquiry was  announced that's the first sign that we  should question this so-called  conventional wisdom of impeachment is  terrible for the markets but Rick Newman  actually says markets would soar if  Trump was impeached because the things  that Donald Trump has done which tend to  boost stocks now this is for better or  worse this is not me saying I agree with  these policies but Trump has done tax  cuts and deregulation very often that  boosts stocks of course there's a it's  it's there's an expense to that as well  which we can talk about those would in  principle stay if Donald Trump were  removed but a lot of the bad things  Trump has done would likely end the  tariffs the xenophobia interfering with  free markets even on things like  immigration immigration is great for the  economy the gains from immigration even  including undocumented immigration are  far greater than the costs to the  economy now this all has a cost to it  right low wages poor conditions for many  legal and illegal immigrants I'm not  making a moral analysis here I'm merely  making an economic one for the sake of  discussion and there is a strong case to  be made that if Donald Trump were  impeached and removed it would not crash  the stock market the way Donald Trump is  pointing out now there's a bigger  argument which is if the right thing to  do for legal reasons on the basis of the  evidence for moral reasons is to impeach  a president you do it you don't make the  calculation that in the short term the  market won't like it so we let a  president remain president who shouldn't  be president that's a separate argument  what we're talking about merely is the  practicality and the logistics of the  market will crash versus the market will  soar if Trump is impeached let me give  you another example the craziness with  Iran what's bad for markets is the  instability with Iran which would  probably calm down if Donald Trump was  removed from office now Rick Newman is  also saying that if Trump were replaced  with pence the economy would be much  better off  I find pence disgusting and it would be  terrible but the main point here that  Rick Newman is making and which i think  is a valid point to make or at least to  propose is that Donald Trump's removal  would not be terrible for the economy as  a foregone conclusion and we need to  challenge all of those ideas merely  because they might have been accurate 20  years ago merely because it seems to  make some intuitive sense does not mean  that we should fall for it hook line and  sinker and more importantly one way or  the other this is not the basis on which  impeachment should be decided upon
 

